{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"limivorous-septic-mouflon","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# `sizing.MODEL_LADDER` hardcodes bare `claude-*` ids, so escalation leaves the configured provider\n\n## The defect\n\n`plan_review/sizing.py` defines the size-escalation ladder as BARE Anthropic model ids:\n\n    MODEL_LADDER = (\n        (\"claude-haiku-4-5\",   200_000),\n        (\"claude-sonnet-4-6\", 1_000_000),\n        (\"claude-opus-4-8\",   1_000_000),\n    )\n\n`models_at_or_above(cfg.model)` returns those bare strings, and `sizing.py:424` — inside\n`pass1_with_ladder`'s escalation loop — does:\n\n    for model in models_at_or_above(cfg.model):\n        passes.pass1_chunk(runner, replace(cfg, model=model), ...)\n\nSo **every context-limit escalation replaces the model with a bare `claude-*` id, consulting no class\ntable and honouring no configured provider.** On a run configured for Bedrock (or any non-default\nprovider), a Pass-1 criterion that trips the context limit silently escalates onto DIRECT ANTHROPIC.\n\n## Why this is exactly the epic's founding defect, still live\n\nEpic 061c exists because \"41 of 42 calls on a real review went to direct Anthropic\" on a\nBedrock-configured run — the batch runners copied `model_ladder[0]` verbatim onto `cfg.model`. Task\n7761 fixed the ENTRY rung by routing it through `resolve_model_string`\n(`production_batch_runner.py`), and the YAML ladder now names classes. **The ESCALATION rungs were\nnever threaded.** They are a second, independent hardcoded-provider path in the same function family.\n\nVerified:\n\n    models_at_or_above('claude-haiku-4-5') -> ['claude-haiku-4-5', 'claude-sonnet-4-6', 'claude-opus-4-8']\n    models_at_or_above('claude-sonnet-4-6') -> ['claude-sonnet-4-6', 'claude-opus-4-8']\n\nall bare, all direct-Anthropic-inferring.\n\n## Interaction with 77ed, which changes the urgency\n\nTicket `77ed` sets the Pass-1 entry rung to `frontier` (restoring opus for finding generation). After\nthat, `models_at_or_above('claude-opus-4-8')` returns just `['claude-opus-4-8']`, so the escalation\nloop becomes a **single iteration at the entry model** and the bare-id substitution\n`replace(cfg, model='claude-opus-4-8')` still executes — replacing a possibly provider-qualified\nmodel with a bare one **even when no escalation is needed**. So 77ed does not make this benign; if\nanything it makes the bare substitution happen on the common path rather than the rare one. Worth\nconfirming that specific interaction as part of the fix.\n\n## Also worth deciding: the ladder conflates two orderings\n\n`MODEL_LADDER` is ordered by CONTEXT WINDOW, but its rungs are model names, and sonnet and opus have\nthe SAME 1M window. So the only size-meaningful step is haiku -> sonnet; sonnet -> opus buys zero\nwindow and is pure cost. A size ladder whose rungs are also a quality ordering cannot express both\ncleanly — the same conflation `77ed` ran into from the other direction. Whether to separate them is a\ndesign question this ticket should at least surface.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a context-limit escalation resolves its target through the model-class vocabulary, so the\n      configured provider is preserved — asserted under a class table configured for a non-default\n      provider, checking the model that reaches the runner\n- [ ] no bare `claude-*` literal remains as the value assigned to `cfg.model` on the escalation path\n- [ ] the window sizes that drive the ESCALATION DECISION remain correct — the fix must not conflate\n      \"which model to escalate to\" with \"how big its context window is\", since the windows are the\n      reason the ladder is ordered as it is\n- [ ] the interaction with `77ed` is checked: with the entry rung at `frontier`, the escalation path\n      must not replace a provider-qualified model with a bare id on the common (non-escalating) path\n- [ ] back-compat: with NO class table configured, every escalation target resolves byte-for-byte to\n      today's value, so existing behaviour is unchanged for unconfigured projects\n- [ ] a test covers the escalation path itself, not just the entry rung — 7761 fixed the entry and\n      this survived, which is the evidence that entry-only tests are insufficient\n\n## Notes\n\nFound while tracing the Pass-1 execution path for `77ed` at the operator's direction. Sibling of\n`afeb` (hand-built sub-calls bypassing the class config) — same defect class, different mechanism:\nthis one bypasses via a hardcoded module constant rather than an inherited `cfg`.","id":"7de4-82f6-fc14-4672","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Pass-1 context-limit escalation sets a BARE Anthropic model id, bypassing the model-class table"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785637423625119001,"uuid":"4e03db6c-36bc-4a67-99cf-f92b5ab72e47"}